<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ardagh, County Longford</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ardagh is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-longford">County Longford</a>, Ireland, whose church is traditionally said to have been founded by Saint Mel, a disciple of Saint Patrick, in the 5th century. It became the seat of the medieval diocese of Ardagh, and the ruins of a small early stone church survive there. The Catholic diocese of Ardagh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clonmacnoise">Clonmacnoise</a> still bears its name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ardagh-county-longford.jpg" alt="Ardagh, County Longford" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laurel Lodged · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Patrick%27s_church,_Ardagh,_County_Longford.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
